BOX('SPECULATIVE MODELING ONLY. NO CLINICAL CLAIMS. NO MEDICAL ADVICE.',
    'This paper reports an architecture testbed: a calibrated toy model used to exercise a relational simulation framework on a public-data problem. '
    'Its numbers are not epidemiology, not risk estimates for any person or group, and not guidance about medication, drugs (prescribed, recreational or psychedelic), music, sleep or seizure management. '
    'Many magnitudes are explicit assumptions. Nothing here should inform treatment decisions; anyone with epilepsy should rely on their own clinicians.')
H('Abstract', 1)
RP([('Background. ', True), (f'About 51.7 million people live with active epilepsy worldwide, roughly two thirds of treated people become seizure-free, and self-reported seizure triggers vary widely between surveys. How triggers, drugs and rare exposures combine is hard to study directly. This study adapts a relational simulation architecture (independent reduced states recombined to match a whole-system observable) to seizure dynamics and asks, as speculative modeling only, what a model calibrated to published numbers implies under explicit assumptions.', False)])
RP([('Methods. ', True), (f'A daily-step stochastic model of virtual brains with 1–4 seizure foci (730 days; outcomes over the final 365 days) was calibrated by Nelder–Mead in four scenarios to {F["nbands"]} published target ranges (seizure freedom, cluster days and survey-reported triggers) and validated on fresh seeds. Eight switchable domain layers, an absorptive buffer (the sponge) and a provisional cell-shading layer were tested. {F["n_cond"]} scenarios covering natural triggers, music, SSRIs, stimulants, NSAIDs, psilocybin and other drugs, and combinations were run with an adaptive stopping rule (95% Wilson half-width ≤ 0.01), for {F["n_persons"] / 1e6:.2f} million simulated people, plus a {F["nohist_persons"] / 1e3:.0f},000-person cohort with no seizure history. Every scenario was reconciled whole-versus-parts and passed a {F["pf_n"]}-check preflight; comparisons are paired with common random numbers.', False)])
RP([('Results. ', True), (f'All {F["n_cond"]} scenarios reached the precision target with zero reconciliation failures. The calibrated model fell inside {vm["nin"]} of {F["nbands"]} public bands: seizure freedom was {pc(vm["sf"])} (95% CI {pc(vm["lo"])}–{pc(vm["hi"])}) against a 60–70% band, and a core model without the extra architecture fit as many or more bands ({v["CORE_anchored"]["nin"]} of {F["nbands"]}). Removing missed medication changed seizure freedom by {ppci(r_miss)} percentage points (pp), stress {ppci(r_stress)} and all triggers {ppci(r_all)}; triggers were not additive. Generic music had no effect and realistic musicogenic prevalence none; in an all-susceptible cohort the share of emotional music drove seizure freedom from {pc(m00.p_sf12)} to {pc(m10.p_sf12)}. SSRI and stimulant results reversed in sign with the evidence base assumed ({ppci(r_ssri_e)} pp versus 0; {ppci(r_st_obs)} versus {ppci(r_st_thr)} pp). NSAID variants stayed within ±{100 * nsaid_max:.2f} pp. High-dose psilocybin exacerbation in people with epilepsy gave {ppci(r_ph)} pp; in people with no seizure history the excess was {nh_e.excess_per100k:.2f} per 100,000 person-years ({nh_e.excess_lo:.1f} to {nh_e.excess_hi:.1f}). Removing the sponge changed seizure freedom by {ppci(r_sp)} pp and removing inhibition by {ppci(r_inh)} pp. In the provisional cell-shading scheme the seizing band held {pc(sh_pos.band3_share)} of brain-days but {pc(sh_pos.levy_band3_share)} of event weight; observational shading changed no outcome, while a coupled variant lowered seizure freedom by up to {abs(100 * r_fb1.d_sf12):.1f} pp.', False)])
RP([('Conclusions. ', True), (f'The testbed reproduces several qualitative public patterns (high residual risk, missed medication as the dominant modifiable trigger, trigger stacking, rarity-limited music effects, evidence-dependent drug results) but fits only {vm["nin"]} of {F["nbands"]} bands quantitatively, and the added architecture does not improve that fit. Conclusions are conditional on assumed mechanisms and magnitudes. No clinical claims are made.', False)])
P('Keywords: epilepsy; seizure triggers; relational simulation; delta reconciliation; stochastic model; model calibration; psilocybin; musicogenic epilepsy; speculative modeling', italic=False, size=9.5, justify=False)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. INTRODUCTION
H('1. Introduction')
P(f'Epilepsy is one of the most common chronic neurological conditions. The Global Burden of Disease 2021 analysis estimated 51.7 million people (95% uncertainty interval 44.9–58.9 million) with active epilepsy, 0.7% of the world population {C("gbd")}, and the WHO fact sheet gives about 50 million, with up to 70% potentially seizure-free if properly diagnosed and treated {C("who")}. A meta-analysis gives active prevalence of 6.38 per 1,000 and incidence of 61.44 per 100,000 person-years {C("fiest17")}. In a treated cohort, 63.7% of 1,795 people were seizure-free for at least a year at last follow-up {C("chen18")}, and 64% of 470 previously untreated people were seizure-free during treatment {C("kwan00")}. The remaining third are the target of the definition of drug-resistant epilepsy {C("ilae")}. A high residual risk is therefore a central fact that any model of seizure dynamics should reproduce.')
P(f'A second regularity is that people with epilepsy report many triggers, but the percentages depend heavily on how they are asked. Single-trigger values for stress range from 30% to 56% and for sleep deprivation from 18% to 52% across surveys {C("frucht", "sperling", "nakken", "nanjing", "aiims", "saudi", "epiwatch")}, whereas only 16.3% of 1,975 patients had any trigger documented in clinic records {C("tert23")}. Prospective diary data suggest modest per-exposure effects, for example an odds ratio of 1.06 per stress point and 0.91 per extra hour of sleep {C("haut07")}. Rare, dramatic triggers coexist with these common ones: photosensitivity in about 3% of people with epilepsy {C("ef")} and musicogenic epilepsy at roughly 1 in 10 million people {C("maguire")}. Finally, the evidence on drugs is mixed or contested: antidepressant studies in the general population and in people with epilepsy point in different directions {C("ssriobs", "ssriepi")}, stimulant use was associated with fewer, not more, acute seizures in one cohort {C("brikell")}, NSAID evidence is sparse and conflicting {C("nsaid")}, and the psychedelic literature consists of reviews, surveys and case reports {C("freidel", "soto", "simonsson")}.')
P('A mathematical model cannot settle these questions, but it can do three useful things: show what each body of evidence implies when put through the same machinery, show which conclusions are fragile to structural assumptions, and expose interactions (for example between triggers) that single-factor surveys cannot. This study uses a relational simulation architecture developed by the author, in which independent reduced states are recombined and must reproduce a whole-system observable exactly. The architecture has been exercised on a metastasis model before ' + C("oroboro", "mca", "v5") + '. Here it is adapted to seizure dynamics and fed with public epilepsy data.')
P('The aims were: (i) to port the architecture and verify that every component is active and that whole-brain results equal the recombined parts; (ii) to calibrate to published targets and report the fit honestly; (iii) to explore natural triggers, music, SSRIs, stimulants, NSAIDs, psilocybin and other drugs, and their combinations, at equal statistical precision; (iv) to quantify which switchable layers change outcomes; and (v) to add a provisional cell-shading representation of focus state and event weight. All of this is speculative modeling; the paper makes no clinical claim.')

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. METHODS
H('2. Methods')
H('2.1 Scope and ground rules', 2)
P(f'The work is a testbed. Only the author\'s own systems were used as the operating architecture: the GitHub repositories under caitlynrandels-del (the Oroboro consciousness-mapping documents and the metastatic-cascade-analysis repository, read through the GitHub connector on 26 November 2026) and the v4 code of the earlier metastasis testbed, from which the random-number generator, framework classes, sponge, layer interface, background processors, update sequence and adaptive stopping rule were ported and re-mapped. No external operating-system or framework assumptions were made; the code uses only numpy, scipy, pandas and matplotlib for arithmetic and plotting. Public data were limited to openly accessible pages, abstracts and CC0 metadata retrieved on 26 November 2026; nothing behind a login or paywall was accessed and no large signal datasets were downloaded. All times are Pacific Time.')
H('2.2 The relational framework and its mapping to seizure dynamics', 2)
P('In the author\'s framework, Genesis is the origin identity to which every reduced state mirrors back; independent Parts hold reduced states and see only their own content plus the previous step\'s summary; Nexus registers, archives and recombines the Parts into whole-system observables; a Transcriber/Dictator keeps a chronicle of critical events; Levy weights score each state transition; a mesh consensus requires the Levy ledger, the heat-trace field and the event matrix to agree; and delta reconciliation requires that the recombined parts equal the whole-system run exactly. A porous sponge (capacity set by HardCapIntegration, permeability as release rate, a membrane as outer skin, Levy as absorbed content and heat-trace as fill gradient) sits between the reduced states and the reconciled whole. Four background processors (principle auditor, memory blender, mesh-reroute monitor, correlation pipeline) run alongside. A six-step update sequence (quick fix, UPDATE ALL, undo, timestamp, do it again, syntax autocorrect) governs every parameter change ' + C("oroboro", "mca") + '. Table 1 gives the mapping used here.')
T(['Framework component', 'Metastasis testbed (v4)', 'Epilepsy testbed (this study)'], [
 ['Genesis', 'primary-tumor shedding stream', 'each virtual brain\'s epileptogenic network; every focus mirrors back to it'],
 ['Independent reduced states (Parts)', 'groups of disseminated cells', 'groups of neural circuits/foci (1–4 per brain, split into K parts by id); a Part sees only its own foci and yesterday\'s whole-brain summary'],
 ['Nexus', 'monthly recombination', 'daily recombination of the Parts into whole-brain observables (seizures, discharges, sponge fill, shade)'],
 ['Levy-weighted transitions (clear +1, detach +7, fail +2, colonize +14, exit +4)', 'clearance, detachment, failed colonization, colonization, dormant exit', 'clear: discharge locally suppressed or focus remits; detach: discharge escapes its focus; fail: escaped discharge fails to recruit the network; colonize: clinical seizure; exit: quiescent focus reawakens'],
 ['Gate with refractoriness', 'establishment checkpoint with load-raised threshold', 'whole-brain recruitment gate, raised for a few days after a seizure (post-ictal refractoriness, lagged)'],
 ['Clearance / dormancy', 'cleared / dormant cells', 'eliminated (all foci cleared, nothing held, seizure-free 12 months: a remission analogue); residual risk (seizure-free 12 months but at least one non-cleared focus or held content); quiescent focus suppressed by medication that can reawaken'],
 ['Overt lesion', 'colonization', 'at least one seizure in the last 12 months (drug-resistance analogue)'],
 ['Mesh consensus', 'ledger vs heat-trace per organ', 'Levy ledger = heat-trace intensity = daily seizure matrix for every focus'],
 ['Delta reconciliation', 'whole vs 4 parts', 'whole-brain run vs K = 2 or 3 independent parts, bit-identical per focus and per day'],
 ['Sponge', 'soaks circulating cells and dormant-exit attempts', 'soaks escaped discharges (+7) before the recruitment gate and reawakening attempts (+4) of quiescent foci; availability from yesterday\'s reconciled fill'],
 ['Cell shading', '—', 'provisional: per-focus shade and cel band; whole-brain shade is the reconciled aggregate (Section 2.7)'],
], 'Mapping of the relational framework onto seizure dynamics.', widths=[1.7, 1.7, 3.6], fs=7.5)
H('2.3 Model dynamics and cohort', 2)
P(f'The model runs in one-day steps for 730 days; every simulated person starts an antiseizure medication (ASM) on day 0, and outcomes are evaluated over the final 365 days. Each virtual brain has 1, 2, 3 or 4 foci with probabilities 0.50, 0.25, 0.15 and 0.10; discharge rates are log-normal across foci (σ = 1.2) and secondary foci have half the base rate. ASM response is two-class: responders (fraction calibrated, near 0.50) have 90–100% suppression and non-responders 20–70%. A discharge is locally suppressed with probability 0.9; an escaped discharge recruits the network, and so becomes a clinical seizure, with probability 0.35. After a seizure the recruitment gate is raised for about a day (post-ictal refractoriness). Quiescent foci reawaken at a low daily rate and foci can remit. Same-day branching (up to 6 generations) gives seizure clusters. Day-level exposures (triggers) multiply the day\'s discharge rate and are person-level, shared by all foci of a brain, so they do not depend on how the foci are partitioned. Missed doses halve efficacy and multiply reawakening by 4 on that day. Outcomes are P(seizure-free in the last 12 months), P(residual risk), P(eliminated), seizures per person-year, cluster days (at least 3 seizures in 24 hours) and the reconciled whole-brain shade. Not modelled: ASM switching, age, epilepsy syndrome, mortality or SUDEP, and treatment gaps in low-income countries.')
P('Self-report is the key bridge to survey data. Surveys ask what people believe triggers their seizures, and the model has no perception data, so two attribution rules were defined: a naive rule (at least one seizure fell on the exposure day or the following day) and a perceived rule (the naive rule and the share of seizure-days in the exposure window at least 1.5 times the share of all days in the window). The ratio 1.5 is an assumption. The perceived rule was used for calibration because the naive rule gave absurd attribution of high-frequency exposures (weather about 45%, music about 89%) in the first model version.')
H('2.4 Public data inputs', 2)
P(f'Tables 2 and 3 list every public input, its licence or terms, and how it was used (T: calibration target; P: parameter taken directly; A: anchor for an assumed parameter; C: comparison or qualitative check; S: scaling). EEG and intracranial EEG hubs (OpenNeuro, NEMAR) were queried for metadata through the public API and S3 bucket; of the SeizeIT2 wearable-EEG dataset (125 subjects, refractory epilepsy, epilepsy monitoring unit) only the 2,850 event-annotation files (0.6 MB) were downloaded, giving {SZ2["seizures"]} annotated seizures in {SZ2["total_hours"]:,.0f} recording hours {C("seizeit2")}. Several figures supplied in the original brief did not match their sources; where they differed the sources\' numbers were run (Table 4).')
